# Connection details for the remote AOSP builder. Sourced by scripts/builder.sh.
#
# Not secret: the host key fingerprint is public, and the private key lives in
# ~/.ssh/palma2pro-builder. Kept in the repo so the wrapper needs no arguments.
#
# BUILDER_HOST and build/ssh/known_hosts are placeholders. Set the address to
# your own build machine, then pin its key:
#     scripts/builder.sh pin <expected-fingerprint>
#
# When the build machine moves or its IP changes, edit BUILDER_HOST and re-pin:
#     scripts/builder.sh pin <expected-fingerprint>

# Machine-specific values. Written as ${VAR:-default} so the environment wins:
#     BUILDER_HOST=my-builder.example scripts/builder.sh ssh
# Keep your own address out of git -- export it in your shell instead.
BUILDER_HOST=${BUILDER_HOST:-CHANGE-ME}
BUILDER_PORT=${BUILDER_PORT:-2222}
BUILDER_USER=builder
BUILDER_KEY=${BUILDER_KEY:-$HOME/.ssh/palma2pro-builder}
BUILDER_TREE=/aosp

# The lunch target. `bp1a` comes from BUILD_ID=BP1A.250505.005, which
# LineageOS/android_build lineage-22.2 declares and e/os/android v4.2-a15 pins.
BUILDER_LUNCH=lineage_Palma2_Pro_C-bp1a-userdebug

# --- Build environment overrides -------------------------------------------
# The currently running container was started from an image that bakes in
# NINJA_HIGHMEM_NUM_JOBS=8 and _JAVA_OPTIONS=-Xmx8g via Dockerfile ENV, so they
# are in the process environment of anything sshd spawns. The Dockerfile no
# longer sets them, but that only takes effect on a rebuild -- and rebuilding now
# would kill the running repo sync. So they are overridden per invocation.
#
# Why unset rather than raise:
#   NINJA_HIGHMEM_NUM_JOBS  caps soong's "highmem" pool (linkers, R8/D8,
#                           metalava). Unset, soong sizes it from available RAM.
#
#                           CORRECTION: "with 46 GB that is a much better number
#                           than 8" was wrong, and this is where it was said. A
#                           droid build died at 3% with
#                               ninja failed with: signal: killed
#                           having been OOM-killed with 35 r8 JVMs live, each
#                           started -JXmx4096M -- about 140 GB of heap ceiling
#                           against 46 GB of RAM. Soong's estimate does not
#                           account for what R8 actually asks for, and the
#                           image's original 8 was nearer the truth than "let
#                           soong decide".
#
#                           Worse, the JVMs outlive the ninja that spawned them:
#                           43 GB was still held by orphans after the build had
#                           failed, so the next attempt would have starved too.
#                           scripts/builder.sh now reaps them before starting and
#                           sets the cap explicitly (HIGHMEM_JOBS, default 6).
#   _JAVA_OPTIONS           applies to EVERY JVM the build starts, and prints a
#                           "Picked up _JAVA_OPTIONS" line to stderr that some
#                           build steps parse as output. Unset, each tool uses
#                           the heap soong assigns it.
#
# BUILDER_UNSET_ENV: names removed from the environment before the build runs.
# BUILDER_SET_ENV:   assignments applied after the removals.
BUILDER_UNSET_ENV="NINJA_HIGHMEM_NUM_JOBS _JAVA_OPTIONS JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS"
#
# NOTE: Docker ENV is invisible over SSH. It populates PID 1's environment, and
# sshd builds a fresh environment per session -- with UsePAM no it does not read
# /etc/environment either. Verified empirically: LANG and USE_CCACHE are unset in
# both login and non-login SSH shells despite the Dockerfile setting them. So
# ccache was silently inactive and the build had no locale. Everything the build
# needs must be listed HERE (or in /etc/profile.d, which the image now writes).
#
# WITH_DEXPREOPT=false: do not AOT-compile on the host at all.
#
# Host dex2oat cannot run correctly under Rosetta. ART's compressed object
# references require the heap mapped below 4 GiB, and x86_64-on-arm64 translation
# does not provide that. Both host binaries fail:
#
#   dex2oatd (debug):  F obj_ptr-inl.h:58 Check failed: ref <= 0xFFFFFFFFU
#                        (ref=140737302179840)      -- a clean DCHECK abort
#   dex2oat  (release): SIGSEGV at 0xf4e73008 in art::InternTable::Table::Find,
#                        registers full of 32-bit-truncated pointers
#                        (rsi/r15 = 0x00000000f4e73000)
#
# So USE_DEX2OAT_DEBUG=false was the wrong fix -- it only changed a checked abort
# into an unchecked crash. The underlying 4 GiB assumption is real.
#
# Disabling dexpreopt means ART JITs at runtime instead of shipping .art/.oat/
# .vdex. Costs: slower first boot and slower app startup. Gains: it builds, and
# the images are smaller. Standard practice for bring-up and for GSI-style builds.
# Revisit once the port boots, ideally by building on a native x86_64 host.
BUILDER_SET_ENV="USE_CCACHE=1 CCACHE_DIR=/aosp/.ccache CCACHE_EXEC=/usr/bin/ccache CCACHE_MAXSIZE=50G LC_ALL=C.UTF-8 LANG=C.UTF-8 WITH_DEXPREOPT=false"
